\begin{abstract}
Training-free LLM information extraction---prompting models with target schemas without fine-tuning---degrades sharply as schema complexity grows: NER recall drops from 0.72 to 0.42 with 64 candidate types, and RE accuracy falls from 85.7\% to 50.4\% with 80 relations. Prior work conflates type count with type confusability, leaving the mechanisms unclear. We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating these factors across three IE tasks and three datasets, introducing the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) and two experimental paradigms: count curves that vary schema size while randomizing confusability, and BCS-controlled experiments that fix size while varying confusability. Our experiments reveal two degradation channels: a count channel that suppresses recall as candidate types increase, and a confusability channel where semantically similar types trigger intra-batch substitution errors---cleanly separable in RE but interacting in NER. Confusability effects amplify $3.4\times$ as schema size grows from 16 to 80 types, with accuracy gaps widening from 9.1 to 31.2 percentage points. Both channels replicate on Llama-3.1-8B (count $r = 0.725$; confusability $\Delta = -27$\,pp), with high-BCS accuracy converging to a model-invariant floor. Our confusability-aware decomposition yields an informative negative result: merge amplification destroys precision across all strategies, identifying a structural bottleneck for decompose-then-merge pipelines in zero-shot IE.
\end{abstract}

